Note the test failures in the trunc mode.

Focused conversion checks
=========================

Build sumi, then run the focused checks from the repository root:

    make sumi
    make -C test_filter check

For Apple Clang and FFTW located through pkg-config:

    make sumi CXX=clang++ \
      CXXFLAGS="-std=c++20 -O3 -Werror -Wall -Wextra -Wshadow -Wpedantic $(pkg-config --cflags fftw3)" \
      FFTW="$(pkg-config --libs fftw3)"
    make -C test_filter check CXX=clang++

The C++ filter checks require a C++20 compiler and no FFTW. The CLI checks use
the compiled sumi executable and Python 3.7 or later (standard library only). The suite
creates check.out and keeps generated samples in memory. The existing dieharder
scripts and saved reports concern the statistical consequences of the filters.

Conversion rules
================

Round, floor, ceil, and trunc operate in floating point first. The resulting
integer-valued double is checked against the signed int range before casting.
The upper bound is exclusive and exactly representable in double. Zero, including
negative zero, converts to integer zero; halfway rounding is away from zero.
For example, INT_MAX + 0.25 is valid with floor and trunc, while ceil rejects it.

Integer/bit output, carry mode, all-statistics reporting, and health tests require
integer conversion. No-output mode (-ot 99) retains this work for the existing
bit-generation benchmarks. Floating-only output bypasses conversion and accepts
finite values outside the int range. Non-finite processed samples are rejected
in all modes; additive overflow is checked on the accumulated value. The process
stops with status 1 before emitting an invalid sample.

Coverage
========

check.cc tests all four filters, positive and negative values, halfway cases,
subnormal values, int endpoints, and neighboring representable floating-point
values. It checks valid results for inputs just outside the raw int range,
rejection of out-of-range filtered results, NaN/infinities, and unknown filters.
The return type remains int. --valid-only runs the representable-result subset.

check.py uses a zero-spectrum generator to supply exact constants through the
actual CLI. It covers integer and packed-bit outputs, negative parity, floating
values beyond the integer range, conversion-dependent options, and failures
during additive/carry processing. Unknown filter codes are rejected even in
floating-only mode. These inputs are deterministic, including exact halfway
values; no zero-width normal distribution is used. Floating text comparisons
allow the existing six-significant-digit rounding (relative tolerance 5e-6).

To run the CLI checks against another executable:

    python3 test_filter/check.py --sumi ./sumi
